IAU 2000B nutation
-----------------

The Rust nutation tables retain the luni-solar multipliers, coefficients,
fundamental arguments and fixed planetary offsets of src/nutation.ts.
That source transcribed the iau2000b function in NOVAS C 3.1, nutation.c,
distributed in novas 3.1.1.5 (source digest in ../LICENSING.md). NOVAS is
a work of the US Government, not subject to copyright. Its authors request
this citation:

  Barron, E. G., Kaplan, G. H., Bangert, J., Bartlett, J. L., Puatua, W.,
  Harris, W., & Barrett, P. (2011), "Naval Observatory Vector Astrometry
  Software (NOVAS) Version 3.1, Introducing a Python Edition," Bull. AAS, 43.

The model is D. D. McCarthy and B. J. Luzum (2003), "An abridged model of
the precession-nutation of the celestial pole," Celestial Mechanics and
Dynamical Astronomy 85, 37-49; IERS Conventions (2003), chapter 5.
The equation of the equinoxes retains the complementary terms of IERS
Conventions (2010), table 5.2e, recorded in the parent's NOTICE.
The mean obliquity and precession follow N. Capitaine, P. T. Wallace and
J. Chapront (2003), "Expressions for IAU 2000 precession quantities,"
Astronomy & Astrophysics 412, 567-586, adopted by IAU 2006 Resolution B1.
No external tables or ephemeris output were fetched for this translation.

Solar and planetary series
-------------------------

The Rust planetary code translates the VSOP evaluation, vector conversion,
light-time and aberration algorithms of astronomy-engine 2.1.19. Its generated
planet_data.rs retains the installed source's Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars,
Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune series coefficients. The Sun's vector
uses the Earth series. No coefficient is fitted or adjusted by this port.

Source: astronomy-engine 2.1.19, esm/astronomy.js, the local pinned npm
dependency. The source header identifies:
